          "lineContent": "_, err = store.InsertEvent(ctx, trajectory.Event{",
          "title": "Insert initial user_request event",
          "description": "First event in trajectory timeline with operation and summary metadata"
        },
        {
          "id": "2f",
          "path": "internal/storage/trajectory/store.go",
          "lineNumber": 213,
          "lineContent": "_, err = s.db.ExecContext(ctx, `\nINSERT INTO trajectories (id, workspace_id, root_request_id, task_ids_json, epic_id, agent_role, job_id, trace_id, status, summary, artifact_digest, outcome_json, created_at, updated_at, session_id)\nVALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)`,",
          "title": "Persist trajectory to SQLite",
          "description": "Stores trajectory with job_id link for correlation with jobs system"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Trajectory Capture Initialization Flow\n├── PrepareJob() in runservice \u003c-- internal/runservice/jobs.go:35\n│   └── trajectorycapture.Start() call \u003c-- 2a\n│       ├── trajectory.Open() \u003c-- 2b\n│       │   └── SQLite DB initialization \u003c-- internal/storage/trajectory/store.go:79\n│       ├── store.InsertUserRequest() \u003c-- 2c\n│       │   └── INSERT INTO user_requests \u003c-- internal/storage/trajectory/store.go:506\n│       ├── store.InsertTrajectory() \u003c-- 2d\n│       │   └── s.db.ExecContext() INSERT \u003c-- 2f\n│       └── store.InsertEvent() \u003c-- 2e\n│           └── INSERT INTO trajectory_events \u003c-- internal/storage/trajectory/store.go:609\n└── RunCapture struct returned \u003c-- internal/trajectorycapture/capture.go:172\n    └── Linked to job via job_id field",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nWhen running skills in agentctl, the system needs to **capture execution traces for training and evaluation** of AI agents. This trajectory capture system records user requests, tool calls, and outcomes in a structured format that can be queried later for optimization purposes. The challenge is initializing this capture **in parallel with job preparation** so that every execution—whether it's a new job or a deduplicated one—has its context properly recorded from the start.\n\n## Details\n\n### Initialization Trigger\n\nTrajectory capture starts during job preparation [2a], immediately after a job ID is assigned. This happens in `PrepareJob()` before the skill actually executes, ensuring the correlation ID and workspace context are captured even if the job fails early.\n\n### Separate Storage Layer\n\nThe trajectory system uses its **own SQLite database** [2b], distinct from the jobs database. This separation allows trajectory data to be queried independently for training purposes without interfering with job execution queries. The store opens at `~/.agentctl/trajectory.db`.\n\n### Three-Record Initialization\n\nTrajectory capture creates three linked records:\n\n1. **UserRequestCapture** [2c] - Normalizes the CLI command and protocol command into a searchable format, extracting task hints like `task_id` and `epic_id` from the input JSON.\n\n2. **Trajectory** [2d] - The main record that links the user request to a job ID, trace ID (correlation ID), and workspace. This record's status gets updated when the job completes [2f].\n\n3. **Initial Event** [2e] - A `user_request` event that marks the start of the trajectory timeline. Subsequent events (tool calls, results) reference this trajectory ID.\n\n### Job Linking\n\nThe trajectory record stores the **job ID** as a foreign key, allowing queries like \"show me all trajectories for jobs that failed\" or \"find trajectories with high human ratings for this skill.\" The correlation ID propagates through the entire execution, linking envelopes, wide events, and trajectory events together.\n\n### Conditional Activation\n\nTrajectory capture only initializes if both `cfg.Storage.Root` and `options.Workspace` are set. If initialization fails, execution continues normally—trajectory capture is best-effort and won't block job execution."
    },
    {
