        },
        {
          "id": "3b",
          "path": "internal/storage/jobs/types/types.go",
          "lineNumber": 81,
          "lineContent": "func ComputeSkillArgsHash(name string, input []byte) string {",
          "title": "Public hash computation entry point",
          "description": "Combines marshaling and hashing into single deterministic operation"
        },
        {
          "id": "3c",
          "path": "internal/storage/jobs/types/types.go",
          "lineNumber": 82,
          "lineContent": "argsBuf := MarshalSkillArgs(name, input)",
          "title": "Marshal skill args to canonical JSON",
          "description": "Creates normalized JSON with skill name, input size, and SHA256 of input"
        },
        {
          "id": "3d",
          "path": "internal/storage/jobs/types/types.go",
          "lineNumber": 69,
          "lineContent": "sum := sha256.Sum256(input)",
          "title": "Hash input bytes for content addressing",
          "description": "Input content hashed separately to avoid storing large payloads in args_json"
        },
        {
          "id": "3e",
          "path": "internal/storage/jobs/types/types.go",
          "lineNumber": 53,
          "lineContent": "h := sha256.New()",
          "title": "Create SHA256 hasher for final digest",
          "description": "Combines command name and canonical args JSON into final args_hash"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Job Preparation Flow\n└── executor.prepareSkillJob() \u003c-- 3a\n    ├── types.MarshalSkillArgs(name, input) \u003c-- internal/storage/jobs/executor/executor.go:148\n    │   ├── Create args map with skill name \u003c-- internal/storage/jobs/types/types.go:65\n    │   ├── sha256.Sum256(input) \u003c-- 3d\n    │   └── json.Marshal(args) to canonical JSON \u003c-- 3c\n    └── types.HashArgs(name, argsBuf) \u003c-- internal/storage/jobs/types/types.go:83\n        ├── h := sha256.New() \u003c-- 3e\n        ├── h.Write([]byte(command)) \u003c-- internal/storage/jobs/types/types.go:54\n        ├── h.Write(argsJSON) \u003c-- internal/storage/jobs/types/types.go:57\n        └── hex.EncodeToString(h.Sum(nil)) \u003c-- internal/storage/jobs/types/types.go:60\n\nPublic API Entry Point\n└── types.ComputeSkillArgsHash(name, input) \u003c-- 3b\n    ├── argsBuf := MarshalSkillArgs() \u003c-- internal/storage/jobs/types/types.go:82\n    └── return HashArgs() \u003c-- internal/storage/jobs/types/types.go:83",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nWhen running skills in agentctl, **deduplication prevents redundant work** by reusing results from identical previous executions. The challenge is determining if two skill invocations are truly identical—same skill name and same input data. A naive string comparison would fail because JSON can be formatted differently while being semantically equivalent. The solution is **deterministic hashing**: compute a SHA256 digest that uniquely identifies each skill invocation, allowing fast database lookups to find duplicate jobs.\n\n## Details\n\n### Hash Computation Pipeline\n\nThe deduplication system uses a two-stage hashing approach [3b]:\n\n1. **Input normalization** [3c]: The skill name and input bytes are marshaled into a canonical JSON structure containing the skill name, input size, and a SHA256 hash of the raw input [3d]. This avoids storing large input payloads in the database while still capturing content identity.\n\n2. **Final hash generation** [3e]: The skill name and canonical JSON are concatenated and hashed with SHA256 to produce the `args_hash` field. This becomes the **deduplication key** stored in the jobs table.\n\n### Why Two Hashes?\n\nThe system hashes the input separately [3d] before hashing again [3e] because:\n- The `args_json` field stores metadata (skill name, input size, input hash) rather than potentially large input payloads\n- The final `args_hash` combines both the skill name and the normalized args for a single lookup key\n- This allows the database index on `args_hash` to efficiently find duplicates without scanning large blobs\n\n### Database Integration\n\nWhen a job is prepared with dedupe enabled [3a], the computed `args_hash` is used in a SQL query to check for existing jobs with the same hash. If found, the existing job is returned; otherwise, a new job is inserted—all within a single transaction to prevent race conditions."
    },
