        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "CLI Run Command Entry\n└── cmd/run.go:executeRunCommand() \u003c-- cmd/agentctl/cmd/run.go:57\n    └── executor.PrepareJob(data) \u003c-- 4a\n        └── runservice/jobs.go:PrepareJob() \u003c-- internal/runservice/jobs.go:35\n            └── jobStore.FindOrPrepareSkillJob() \u003c-- internal/runservice/jobs.go:39\n                └── jobs/store.go:FindOrPrepareSkillJob() \u003c-- internal/storage/jobs/store.go:196\n                    └── [returns existing job, isDup=true] \u003c-- internal/storage/jobs/store.go:198\n                        └── CLI detects duplicate \u003c-- 4a\n                            └── executor.HandleDuplicate(job) \u003c-- 4b\n                                ├── Print \"using existing job\" \u003c-- internal/runservice/jobs.go:86\n                                └── jobStore.WaitForCompletion() \u003c-- 4c\n                                    └── jobs/store.go:WaitForCompletion() \u003c-- internal/storage/jobs/store.go:226\n                                        └── Poll loop with backoff \u003c-- internal/storage/jobs/store.go:235\n                                            └── persist.Get(jobID) \u003c-- 4d\n                                                └── Check if terminal state \u003c-- internal/storage/jobs/store.go:248\n                                                    └── [job completed] \u003c-- internal/storage/jobs/store.go:250\n                                                        └── jobStore.Result(job.ID) \u003c-- 4e\n                                                            └── Read result.json \u003c-- internal/storage/jobs/store.go:127\n                                                                └── HandleResult() \u003c-- 4f",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nWhen users run the same skill with identical inputs multiple times, **re-executing the skill wastes time and resources**. The dedupe system solves this by detecting duplicate requests using SHA256 hashing and **reusing existing job results** instead of re-running the skill [4a]. This is critical for hooks that may be invoked repeatedly with identical inputs during development workflows.\n\nThe challenge: when a duplicate is detected, the **existing job might still be running** from a previous invocation. The system must wait for that job to complete before returning its result, rather than executing the same work twice concurrently.\n\n## Motivation\n\nWhen users run the same skill with identical inputs multiple times (common in hooks or automated workflows), re-executing is wasteful. The **dedupe system** uses SHA256 hashing of skill name + input to detect duplicates and reuse existing job results [4a]. When a duplicate is found, instead of running the skill again, the system **waits for the original job to complete** and returns its cached result [4b].\n\nThis solves two problems:\n1. **Avoids redundant computation** - If the same skill with identical input is already running or completed, reuse that work\n2. **Prevents race conditions** - Multiple concurrent requests with identical inputs share a single execution rather than spawning duplicate jobs\n\n## Motivation\n\nWhen multiple processes or hooks invoke the same skill with identical inputs (e.g., git hooks running in parallel), **deduplication prevents wasteful re-execution**. The system uses SHA256 hashing of skill name + input to identify duplicates [1a]. When a duplicate is detected [4a], instead of running the skill again, the system waits for the original job to complete [4c] and returns its cached result [4e].\n\nThis is critical for **hook scenarios** where multiple git operations might trigger the same skill invocation concurrently—dedupe ensures only one execution happens while others wait and reuse the result.\n\n## Motivation\n\nWhen multiple processes or requests invoke the same skill with identical inputs (e.g., parallel git hooks running `agentctl run hooks/pre-commit`), executing the skill multiple times wastes resources and creates race conditions. **Job deduplication** solves this by detecting duplicate work via SHA256 hashing [3a] and reusing existing job results [4a].\n\nThe duplicate handling flow ensures that when a job with identical inputs already exists, the system **waits for that job to complete** [4c] rather than re-executing the skill, saving compute time and ensuring consistent results.\n\n## Motivation\n\nWhen multiple processes invoke the same skill with identical inputs (common in git hooks or parallel CI runs), executing redundantly wastes time and resources. The **dedupe system** uses SHA256 hashing of skill name + input to detect duplicates [1f] and reuse existing job results [4f]. This is critical for **hook execution** where multiple concurrent git operations might trigger identical skill invocations.\n\n## Details\n\n**Duplicate detection** happens during job preparation [4a]. When `--dedupe` is enabled, `FindOrPrepareSkillJob` performs an **atomic find-or-insert** using a transaction that queries for existing jobs by `args_hash` [1f]. If a duplicate is found, the boolean flag `isDuplicate=true` is returned [4a].\n\nThe **duplicate handling path** [4b] differs from normal execution: instead of running the skill again, it waits for the existing job to complete [4c]. The wait loop uses **exponential backoff** (500ms → 10s max) to reduce database load [4d]. Once the job reaches a terminal state (ok/error/canceled), the system reads the cached `result.json` file [4e] and returns it directly [4f].\n\nThis **avoids redundant execution** when multiple concurrent processes (like parallel git hooks) invoke the same skill with identical inputs. The dedupe mechanism uses SHA256 hashing of skill name + input to detect duplicates atomically in a SQLite transaction [1f]."
    },
