      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Synchronous Execution with Trajectory Capture\n├── CLI Command Entry\n│   └── executor.ExecuteSync(job) \u003c-- 5a\n│       └── Runservice Jobs Layer\n│           ├── jobStore.ExecutePreparedSkill() \u003c-- 5b\n│           │   └── Jobs Store Layer\n│           │       └── executor.ExecutePrepared() \u003c-- 5c\n│           │           └── Jobs Executor Layer\n│           │               ├── persist.UpdateState(running) \u003c-- internal/storage/jobs/executor/executor.go:284\n│           │               ├── runSkill() invocation \u003c-- 5d\n│           │               │   └── skillExecutor.Execute() \u003c-- internal/storage/jobs/executor/executor.go:318\n│           │               │       └── [skill binary runs]\n│           │               └── handleSuccess() \u003c-- internal/storage/jobs/executor/executor.go:251\n│           │                   └── persist.UpdateState(ok) \u003c-- internal/storage/jobs/executor/executor.go:394\n│           └── HandleResult(jobID, result) \u003c-- 5e\n│               └── Runservice Result Handler\n│                   ├── enforceOutputLimit() \u003c-- internal/runservice/result.go:23\n│                   ├── handleArtifacts() \u003c-- internal/runservice/result.go:25\n│                   ├── trajCapture != nil check \u003c-- 5f\n│                   │   └── trajCapture.CaptureResult() \u003c-- 5g\n│                   │       └── [trajectory event insert]\n│                   ├── PersistCache() \u003c-- internal/runservice/result.go:42\n│                   └── writeEnvelope(stdout) \u003c-- internal/runservice/result.go:52",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nWhen users run skills (executable tools) in agentctl, the system needs to **avoid redundant work** by detecting when the same skill with identical inputs has already been executed. Additionally, for training AI agents, the system must **capture detailed execution traces** showing what happened during each run—user requests, tool calls, and results—all linked together by correlation IDs.\n\nThe challenge is coordinating two independent systems: the **jobs system** (which handles execution and deduplication) and the **trajectory capture system** (which records training data), ensuring they work together seamlessly without blocking each other.\n\n## Details\n\n### Synchronous Execution Flow\n\nWhen a non-duplicate job executes synchronously [5a], the system follows a layered delegation pattern. The CLI executor calls into the runservice layer [5b], which delegates to the jobs store [5c], which finally invokes the executor that manages the actual skill binary execution [5d].\n\n### State Management During Execution\n\nBefore running the skill, the executor updates the job state to **\"running\"** in the SQLite database to track progress. The actual skill execution happens via `skillExecutor.Execute()`, which spawns the skill binary as a subprocess and captures stdout/stderr. After successful execution, the state transitions to **\"ok\"** with the result path recorded.\n\n### Result Processing Pipeline\n\nOnce execution completes, `HandleResult()` [5e] orchestrates several critical post-processing steps in a **specific order**:\n\n1. **Output limiting** [5f] runs first—if the result exceeds the configured size limit (default 32KB), it's stored in content-addressed storage (CAS) and replaced with a digest reference\n2. **Artifact handling** extracts and pins any CAS digests from the result to prevent garbage collection\n3. **Trajectory capture** [5g] records the result as an event in the trajectory database, but only if trajectory capture was initialized (requires storage root and workspace configuration)\n4. **Cache persistence** stores the result for future deduplication\n5. **Output emission** writes the final envelope to stdout\n\n### Trajectory Capture Integration\n\nTrajectory capture is **optional and non-blocking**—if it fails, execution continues [5g]. The capture system records the result envelope as a `tool_result` or `hook_result` event, extracts summary data and artifact digests, determines the trajectory outcome (ok/error) based on envelope status, and updates the trajectory record with final status and metadata for later optimization queries.\n\n### Key Design Decisions\n\nThe trajectory capture system uses a **separate SQLite database** from the jobs database, allowing independent evolution and preventing job execution from being blocked by trajectory operations. The result processing order is critical: output limiting must happen before artifact handling to ensure truncation digests get pinned and don't become dangling references."
    },
    {
      "id": "6",
      "title": "Trajectory Result Event Capture and Status Update",
      "description": "Trajectory capture system records execution result as event and updates trajectory status",
      "locations": [
        {
